            <ArticleTitle>Aseptic meningoencephalitis in adults: liberal or restrictive treatment?</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>101-4</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>39 adult patients suffering from aseptic meningoencephalitis were admitted to hospital during April-December 1976 and were divided into 2 groups, according to birth date. One group (n = 24) was treated with bed rest in the febrile stage and recommended to avoid for some weeks activities that promote headache. The other group (n = 15) was encouraged to resume daily life as soon as possible. No pertinent difference was found between the groups in admission. The patients were followed for 9 months by questionnaires and registration of sick leave. The average number of days away from work was 32 in the &quot;restrictive&quot; and 15 in the &quot;liberal&quot; group. No untoward effects were found from a shorter sick leave and a rapid resumption of normal physical activity.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
